  "abstract_source": "https://github.com/bwuebben/small-dolbeault-eigenvalues/blob/75f5bae32abcec8006165eee8bff5306fb78e5ff/paper3/paper/main.tex",
  "abstract_html": "<p>We determine the small spectrum of the Dolbeault Laplacian on trace-free endomorphisms of Hermitian–Einstein bundles near intersections of slope-stability walls. On a compact Kähler manifold of dimension at least two, consider an extension of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x02A01;</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, where the factors are pairwise nonisomorphic and stable of equal slope at the limiting Kähler class, and every extension component is nonzero. Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>rk</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the Einstein constant of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> minus that of the extension. In the chamber <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> eigenvalues tend to zero. They agree, to relative error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the eigenvalues of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>diag</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. The estimate is uniform in all positive ratios of the slope gaps. Thus the ranks and slopes determine the leading spectrum even when the gaps vanish at arbitrarily different rates.</p><p>For two factors we obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is the quotient Einstein constant minus the subbundle Einstein constant and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is given by the Green operator of the graded connection. We also determine the first eigensection through order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> in every spatial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> norm. Toric tangent bundles provide examples with arbitrarily many independently varying small eigenvalues.</p>",
